WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.867
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:02.200
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Hi, everyone.

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:04.870
I'm Priyanka Vergadia, Developer
Advocate on the Google Cloud

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:05.370
team.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:08.430
And today, I'm going to show
you how to use Vertex AI

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:11.280
and help a friend get into yoga.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.830
We are building a yoga
pose classification model.

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:16.059
Let's get started.

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:18.660
First thing we need is the data.

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:20.820
For this step, my
friend Sara and I

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.340
collected a bunch of
images from a yoga practice

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.670
and uploaded them into
the managed data sets.

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:30.360
As you see here, the images
are labeled in five categories.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.230
I can even upload unlabeled
images and label them.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.120
We can analyze our
data set to see

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.960
that we have enough
examples of each categories.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.780
Now, we are ready
to train our model.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:46.500
In here, I'm selecting
AutoML for training method.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.170
You can also train a model
to deploy on the Edge

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.200
or on Premise, or use custom
training if you're writing

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.870
your own custom model code.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:58.390
Now, we define our model.

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:00.580
AutoML automatically
splits the data

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.830
into training,
validation, and test.

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:06.500
But we can change
it if you want to.

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:10.490
In Compute and pricing, we get
to select how many node hours

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:12.950
we want to train the model.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:16.280
At this point, we wait
for the model to train.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.890
Once it is trained, we
see it in the Model tab

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:22.290
and can evaluate the model.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.540
We can see our model's
average precision.

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:29.210
It ranges from 0 to 1, where a
higher value indicates a higher

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:32.140
quality model.

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:33.840
We can see that the
confusion matrix

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.650
helps us understand where our
model is confusing two results.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:39.630
Now that we have
evaluated our model,

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:43.020
it's time to deploy
it to an endpoint.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.840
We can split traffic
between two models deployed

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:49.140
on the same endpoint if needed.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.680
Allocate the number of nodes
needed for this endpoint.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:54.630
Depending on where
your users are,

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:57.450
select a region to
deploy your endpoint.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:58.370
And that's it.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:01.020
Hit Deploy, and we
have an endpoint.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.540
Now, we can test our
endpoint and the model

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:06.100
by uploading an image.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.900
And yay, it works.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:10.860
You can also make
predictions using the SDK.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.320
If this has peaked your
interest in Vertex AI

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.050
and you want to learn more,
head over to the documentation

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.137
linked on screen.

00:02:17.137 --> 00:02:17.970
Thanks for watching.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:21.020
[MUSIC PLAYING]

